    why: "A post-tool rule, not a verdict on an action: the tool already ran. Unicode tag characters, runs of variation selectors, dense zero-width characters (eight or more, or four in a row; one on its own is ordinary text, as pasted prose has them, and no finding), direction-changing characters and terminal control sequences (concealed text, cursor moves and erases, overwriting carriage returns, OSC links, titles and clipboard writes) make a text read one way to the model and another to a person. Where the firewall can rewrite what the agent receives (the MCP gateway, Claude Code for MCP results and fetched pages), each run of invisible characters is replaced by a visible marker and every control sequence is removed; where it cannot (Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor, the library, and Claude Code for file reads and shell output), the session is marked untrusted and the agent is warned. Text smuggled in tag characters or variation selectors makes the session untrusted everywhere, and so does a dense run of zero-width characters in the hooks and the library, which do not rewrite it; the gateway marks such a run and withholds the result only when a phrase addressed to an AI assistant is within 200 characters of it. Colours and bold in a program's output are ordinary and pass.",
